# History: this file briefly suppressed CVE-2026-5773 and CVE-2026-6276
# (curl/libcurl, HIGH) while the fix (curl 8.20.0-r0+) lagged Alpine's
# v3.23 stable branch. Resolved 2026-07-17 by bumping the base image to
# the Alpine 3.24 line (ghcr.io/linuxserver/baseimage-alpine:3.24-03b33b49-ls6),
# which ships curl 8.21.0-r0 and does not need the ignore. See the FROM
# comment in the Dockerfile for the full record.
#
# 2026-09-08: GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 (msgpack) and CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools)
# are pip 26.2.1's OWN internal vendored copies -- pip/_vendor/msgpack==1.1.2
# and pip/_vendor/setuptools==70.3.0 (confirmed against pip's vendor.txt at
# that exact tag). (The image no longer installs top-level msgpack or
# setuptools at all as of 2026-10-29; see the venv RUN line in the
# Dockerfile.) Not reachable at
# runtime: pip is only used during the image build, never invoked by the
# running app (same Dockerfile comment already establishes this for pip
# itself). No pip release has bumped these vendor pins yet as of this date.
# Re-check next time this file changes; drop these two lines once a pip
# release updates its vendored msgpack/setuptools past the fixed versions.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273

# 2026-09-09: CVE-2026-59890 (setuptools, MANIFEST.in exclusion bypass via
# NFC/NFD normalization collision) is the SAME pip/_vendor/setuptools==70.3.0
# as CVE-2025-47273 above, just a second CVE against that one vendored copy --
# not a top-level `setuptools` (the image installs none). Still
# pip 26.2.1 as of this date; reconfirmed against that tag's vendor.txt,
# which still pins setuptools==70.3.0. Same "not reachable at runtime"
# reasoning applies. Drop this line once a pip release bumps the vendored
# setuptools past 83.0.0.
# Re-checked 2026-10-29: pip 26.2.2 (latest) still vendors msgpack 1.1.2 and
# setuptools 70.3.0, so all three entries above remain valid.
CVE-2026-59890

# 2026-10-29: CVE-2026-114613 (msgpack, map-deserialization memory-leak DoS,
# fixed 1.2.3) and CVE-2026-97687 / CVE-2026-97689 (urllib3, HTTPS-proxy TLS
# override and chunk-parser memory DoS, fixed 2.8.0) are pip 26.2.2's OWN
# vendored copies -- pip/_vendor/msgpack==1.1.2 and pip/_vendor/urllib3==2.7.0
# per that tag's vendor.txt. Trivy reports them under a bare "Python" target,
# not a site-packages dist-info. The top-level copies the app actually imports
# are current and scanned clean in the same run (msgpack 1.2.4, urllib3 2.8.0);
# no top-level msgpack is installed at all (nothing depends on it) and urllib3 comes in
# via requests, which pip-audit gates in CI, so ignoring these IDs here can't
# hide a regression in the runtime copies. Same "pip is build-time only, never
# invoked by the running app" reasoning as the entries above. 26.2.2 is the
# newest pip as of this date. Drop these three lines once a pip release
# vendors msgpack >= 1.2.3 and urllib3 >= 2.8.0.
CVE-2026-114613
CVE-2026-97687
CVE-2026-97689

# 2026-10-29: two lower-severity CVEs against the SAME pip 26.2.2 vendored
# copies as the entries above. They never trip the CRITICAL/HIGH gate, but
# the SARIF upload includes every severity (trivy-action ignores `severity:`
# for SARIF unless limit-severities-for-sarif is set), so they showed up as
# code-scanning alerts #307 and #304:
# - CVE-2026-97688 (MEDIUM): pip/_vendor/urllib3 2.7.0, chunked Deflate
#   decoding DoS, fixed 2.8.0. Top-level urllib3 is 2.8.0 and scans clean.
# - GHSA-xmj3-9gf7-h52w (LOW): pip/_vendor/setuptools 70.3.0, Windows path
#   escape via namespace_packages.txt during legacy wheel install, fixed
#   84.1.0. Windows-only, and the image is Linux.
# Same "pip is build-time only" rationale. Drop with the other pip-vendored
# entries once a pip release vendors fixed versions.
CVE-2026-97688
GHSA-xmj3-9gf7-h52w
